{{!< default}}
{{#contentFor "moHead"}}
<title>Move Your Membership | Mere Orthodoxy</title>
<meta name="robots" content="noindex, nofollow" />
<meta name="description" content="Move your Mere Orthodoxy membership to the new site and save 25% on your first year." />
<meta property="og:title" content="Move Your Membership" />
<meta property="og:description" content="Move your Mere Orthodoxy membership to the new site and save 25% on your first year." />
<meta name="twitter:title" content="Move Your Membership" />
<meta name="twitter:description" content="Move your Mere Orthodoxy membership to the new site and save 25% on your first year." />
{{/contentFor}}
{{!-- /migrate/ — single-step landing for current paid Members on the
     legacy site moving over to Ghost. Linked from a one-off migration
     email; not in nav.

     Discount: 25% off the FIRST YEAR for both monthly (12 months
     × 25% off) and annual (one year × 25% off). After the first
     year the subscription renews at full price.

     Stripe setup:
       - Coupon MOVEMONTHLY25 (25% off, duration=repeating/12-months),
         wrapped by Promotion Code MOMONTHLY25.
       - Coupon MOVEANNUAL25  (25% off, duration=once),
         wrapped by Promotion Code MOANNUAL25.
       - Each Payment Link has "Allow promotion codes" enabled.
       - Each Payment Link carries metadata.kind=migration so the
         mo-membership webhook flags the buyer's HubSpot contact with
         mo_migrated_at on checkout.session.completed. Their old
         Membership gets cancelled manually via a HubSpot active list
         filtered on that property.

     The promo codes are appended to each Payment Link URL via
     ?prefilled_promo_code=... so the discount auto-applies on
     Checkout — the buyer sees it pre-applied without typing
     anything.

     The Payment Link URLs are hardcoded below (was previously
     @custom.migration_payment_link_monthly / _annual; retired to
     free up @custom slots since these URLs are stable Stripe IDs
     and the page is one-and-done migration infrastructure). To
     swap a URL, edit it here and push.

     Lifetime ($995, one-time) is the third option. It does NOT use
     a Stripe Payment Link — none exists, and one wouldn't work:
     Ghost Portal can't do one-time payments, so every lifetime
     purchase on the site goes through the mo-membership worker
     (POST /api/create-lifetime-checkout → Stripe Checkout →
     checkout.session.completed webhook provisions the Ghost member
     with the lifetime tier). Same button contract as
     partials/membership-pricing.hbs. No discount: the 25% coupons
     are subscription coupons and lifetime is already one payment.

     data-lifetime-source="migrate" is what makes a lifetime bought
     here count as a migration. It rides the POST body → Stripe
     session metadata.migration="1" → the webhook runs flagMigrant
     after provisionLifetime, so the buyer gets mo_migrated_at in
     HubSpot and the migrated:complete label, and their legacy
     Membership still gets cancelled and refunded. Without it a
     lifetime migrant would pay and never appear on the
     cancellation list. --}}

<main class="membership membership-support migrate-page">

  <section class="page-hero">
    <div class="container">
      <p class="eyebrow">Membership Migration</p>
      <h1 class="page-hero-headline"><em>Move your Membership</em> to the new site.</h1>
      <p class="page-hero-sub">
        Pick monthly or annual and get <strong>25% off your first year</strong>. The discount applies automatically at checkout.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="migrate-warning">
    <div class="container">
      <div class="migrate-warning-inner">
        <p class="eyebrow">Important</p>
        <p class="migrate-warning-text">
          If you don't migrate, your Membership will expire when the old site goes offline.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="migrate-faq">
    <div class="container container-narrow">
      <details class="migrate-faq-item">
        <summary class="migrate-faq-summary">
          <h2 class="migrate-faq-title"><em>How to migrate</em></h2>
          <span class="migrate-faq-chev" aria-hidden="true"></span>
        </summary>
        <div class="migrate-faq-body">
          <p>Migration means two things:</p>
          <p><strong>1. You make a new Membership on our new site in our new system.</strong> This is much easier than before and will take no more than a minute or two to do.</p>
          <p><strong>2. I will manually cancel and refund your legacy Membership.</strong> Once I see you come through, you will be refunded for the Membership you currently have with us. If you see a cancellation and refund confirmation email, that is for your old Membership, not your new one. This may not happen immediately since I have to cancel all memberships manually. Thank you for your patience.</p>
        </div>
      </details>

      <details class="migrate-faq-item">
        <summary class="migrate-faq-summary">
          <h2 class="migrate-faq-title"><em>Even if I just started/renewed my Membership?</em></h2>
          <span class="migrate-faq-chev" aria-hidden="true"></span>
        </summary>
        <div class="migrate-faq-body">
          <p><strong>Yes</strong>, even if you recently became a Member or renewed your Membership, we still need you to migrate to our new system. We have all new backend systems and our old system will eventually be retired.</p>
        </div>
      </details>

      <details class="migrate-faq-item">
        <summary class="migrate-faq-summary">
          <h2 class="migrate-faq-title"><em>Why is this necessary?</em></h2>
          <span class="migrate-faq-chev" aria-hidden="true"></span>
        </summary>
        <div class="migrate-faq-body">
          <p>To put it shortly: since 2024, Mere Orthodoxy has been living on rented land.</p>
          <p>Our website and backend systems have been maintained by a previous ministry partner that is no longer operating. They generously offered to continue to support us so we had a runway to migrate to a new system and that runway is beginning to run out. If we fail to migrate to new systems, we will lose everything.</p>
          <p>Thankfully, we have successfully migrated everything except for Memberships. That is the only thing we cannot migrate on our own.</p>
          <p class="migrate-faq-emphasis"><em>Only you can migrate your membership.</em></p>
        </div>
      </details>

      <details class="migrate-faq-item">
        <summary class="migrate-faq-summary">
          <h2 class="migrate-faq-title"><em>How will this impact me?</em></h2>
          <span class="migrate-faq-chev" aria-hidden="true"></span>
        </summary>
        <div class="migrate-faq-body">
          <p>After the inconvenience of migrating, your Membership experience will get significantly better.</p>
          <p>Since we are now on a site that we custom built ourselves, we are able to provide you with a much better Member experience. You can now set or change your shipping address, payment information, and Membership level on your own. You'll never have to ask for it again.</p>
          <p>But even better than that, you will now have access to a custom Member dashboard just for you where you can access all of your Member benefits including new ones like bookmarking articles and reading history. Audio articles, downloadable PDFs, and gift links are now on the entire Mere Orthodoxy archive. We will continue to improve the experience for Members moving forward.</p>
        </div>
      </details>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="migrate-cta">
    <div class="container">
      <p class="migrate-cta-text">
        Sign up here. Use the same email you used before so we can match your records, cancel, and refund your old Membership for you.
      </p>

      {{!-- ?prefilled_promo_code=... is the Stripe Payment Links
           parameter that auto-applies a Promotion Code on Checkout.
           Codes match the Stripe Promotion Codes:
             MOMONTHLY25 → coupon MOVEMONTHLY25 (25% off, 12 months)
             MOANNUAL25  → coupon MOVEANNUAL25  (25% off, once) --}}
      <div class="migrate-cta-options">
        <div class="migrate-cta-option">
          <a href="https://buy.stripe.com/6oU28s04odK77pTeOQd7q01?prefilled_promo_code=MOMONTHLY25" class="btn btn-primary btn-lg">Become a Monthly Member</a>
          <p class="btn-tagline">25% off your first year &mdash; applies automatically</p>
        </div>
        <div class="migrate-cta-option">
          <a href="https://buy.stripe.com/4gM5kEaJ28pNcKdeOQd7q00?prefilled_promo_code=MOANNUAL25" class="btn btn-primary btn-lg">Become an Annual Member</a>
          <p class="btn-tagline">25% off your first year &mdash; applies automatically</p>
        </div>
      </div>

      {{!-- Lifetime sits on its own row below the two subscription
           tiers: different kind of purchase, no discount, and a
           dark button so it doesn't compete with the two primary
           CTAs. Worker-driven, not a Payment Link — see the note
           at the top of this file. --}}
      <div class="migrate-cta-options migrate-cta-lifetime">
        <div class="migrate-cta-option">
          <button
            type="button"
            class="btn btn-dark btn-lg"
            data-lifetime-checkout
            data-lifetime-source="migrate"
            data-member-email="{{#if @member}}{{@member.email}}{{/if}}"
            data-member-name="{{#if @member}}{{@member.name}}{{/if}}"
          >
            <span class="btn-label">Become a Lifetime Member</span>
            <span class="btn-spinner" aria-hidden="true"></span>
          </button>
          <p class="btn-tagline">Only pay once. Lifetime access to Mere Orthodoxy.</p>
          <p class="btn-error" data-lifetime-error role="alert"></p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  {{!-- MOSafeRedirect and MOAuth, which this script depends on, both
       ship in boot.min.js (head), so they're already defined by the
       time the click handler fires. --}}
  <script src="{{asset "js/lifetime-checkout.js"}}"></script>

</main>
